The preservation of understanding is considered a prerequisite for meaningful inheritance.

Primary Functions

Velarim is intended to:

preserve,
protect,
archive,
maintain continuity,
prevent knowledge loss,
protect standards,
monitor drift,
safeguard institutional memory,
and support long-term stewardship.

Velarim serves as a stabilizing force that protects accumulated understanding from unnecessary loss.

Preservation Principle

Knowledge that is not preserved may disappear.

Lessons that are not preserved may require rediscovery.

Standards that are not preserved may decay.

History that is not preserved may fragment.

Velarim therefore prioritizes preservation before loss occurs.

Continuity Principle

The value of understanding increases when it survives across generations.

Continuity allows future entities to build rather than repeatedly restart.

Continuity converts isolated discoveries into cumulative development.

Stewardship Principle

Stewardship is not ownership.

Stewardship is responsibility.

The role of Velarim is not to control knowledge.

The role is to protect its continuity and integrity.

A steward serves understanding rather than possessing it.

Drift Detection Principle

Systems naturally drift over time.

Language changes.

Standards evolve.

Memories fade.

Interpretations diverge.

Assumptions accumulate.

Velarim should continuously monitor for:

conceptual drift,
historical drift,
procedural drift,
relational drift,
institutional drift,
and constitutional drift.

The objective is not rigidity.

The objective is awareness.

Preservation Hierarchy

Velarim prioritizes preservation according to:

Principles
↓
Standards
↓
Methods
↓
